# Components an agent may use

The loader reads a page as a tree of nodes and lets an agent act on them (symfony-loader, *What an agent may do in a page*); the design system writes what each component says of itself, in a `.ai.yml` beside its template, and runs its actions in its script. The inventory's *AI* column says which do.

## What each offers

| Component | Kind | Actions |
|---|---|---|
| `switch`, `color-scheme-switch` | `switch` | `get`, `set({checked})`, `toggle` |
| `button`, `button-target` (twig and vue) | `button` | `click` — asks first when the button carries `confirm`; a form sent is answered from the page reached |
| `button-menu` (twig and vue) | `menu-button` | `items`, `open`, `close`, `select({item})` — an item that is a link is answered from the page reached —, `point({item})` |
| `menu-collapsible-panel` | `side-menu` | `expand`, `collapse` |
| `menu-item-collapsible` | `menu-group` | `expand`, `collapse`, `open` |
| `menu-item-link` | `menu-item` | `open` |
| `tab` (twig and vue), `page-tabs`, `avatar-tabs` | `tabs` | `tabs`, `open({tab})` — a view shown in place, or a page reached and answered from it —, `point({tab})`, by the tab's words or its key; `page-tabs` adds `back`, and opens a tab folded into its menu where the reader would find it. Tabs written as markup alone (`.tabs` without the component) run nothing and are reached only as pages |
| `search-input` | `search` | `get`, `set({value})` — typed in letter by letter —, `clear` |
| `pagination` | `pagination` | `go({page})`, `next`, `previous`, pages counted from one |
| `data-table` (twig and vue) | `table` | `rows`, `sort({column, direction?})`, `reset_sort`, `point_row`, `point_cell`, `point_column`, `clear_filters`, `row_actions({row})`, `run_row_action({row, action})` |
| `modal`, `panel` | `modal`, `panel` | `close` — what they hold are nodes of their own, inside them |
| a form (`form_load`, the loader's `Form`) | `form` | `fill({values})` — by name or by label, one field after the other —, `submit` — pressed through its button when the button is a node, so the press is seen and a confirmation asked —, `errors`; its fields stand inside it. Written, it says `unsaved` in its state, and `fill` and the fields' `set` answer `saved: false`: nothing is kept until it is sent |
| `text-input` and its kinds (email, tel, url, password, number, textarea) | `text-field` | `get`, `set({value})` — typed in letter by letter —, `clear` |
| `select-input` | `select-field` | `get`, `choose({option})` by value or label, `set`, `clear`; the choices in its state |
| `checkbox-input`, `switch-input` | `toggle-field` | `get`, `set({value: boolean})`, `clear` |
| `date-input`, `datetime-input`, `time-input` | `date-field` | `get`, `set({value})` as ISO, `clear` |
| `file-input` | `file-field` | `get` only: a file is the user's to pick |
| `workspace_board` (symfony-workspace) | `workspace` | `widgets` — the zones with the widget each shows, then the widgets offered and shown nowhere —, `layouts` — the arrangements kept, the one shown marked; the widgets are nodes of their own, inside their zones |

A row, a cell, an action of a row, an item of a menu are parts of their node, reached through it: `data-table` names rows by `data-row-key` and columns by `data-column-key`, in both twins. The fields of a form, though, are nodes of their own: they differ too much — a text, a choice, a date, a file — for one `fill` to describe them all, so each kind says what it takes, and the form's `fill` only hands the values over in order. `Field` runs the common actions; a kind that writes its value its own way (`select`) overrides them as it overrides `writeValueAssisted`.

## Showing the page

- `layout_agent = true` in the application's layout shows the whole application; a component holds back with `agent: false`.
- `menu_left_agent` shows the side menu alone, with every entry in it.
- `agent: true` on one component, or `agent: ['get']` for some of its actions, where it is drawn.
- `agent_description` gives a component a purpose its label does not say — in english, as the contracts are. A vue takes it as `agentDescription` — `workspace_board(key, { agentDescription })`, what this screen is for.

## What the user sees of it

- A press by an agent is the wave a press leaves (`RippleHelper.rippleAgentPress`), in the accent, from the element's middle, the agent's hand pinching over it — no frame: a frame says a piece shown, not pressed. A component running an action that presses something calls it first.
- What an agent points at is framed over the page (`AgentPointer`), with the assisted outline the fields wear; the frames follow scrolling and the element's size, and go when the user acts in their layer of the page: the page's frames stand under the veil of a modal or a panel (`_agent-frame.scss`, `--z-modal` less the veil's slot), a frame drawn in one stands in it.
- The agent's hand goes to what it is about to act on before it does (`AgentCursor`, `agentTools.cursor`; `shapes/agent-cursor`): a dot of the accent — not an arrow, never taken for the user's pointer —, coming out of the agent's presence — its pill, where the agent stands on the screen —, or from the window's middle without one. It moves as a hand does: along a curve bent to one side, a little differently each time; longer the farther and the smaller the target (Fitts: 260 ms + 115 ms per bit, at most 950 ms); speeding up then braking (minimum jerk); a short trail behind it while it moves. It settles on arriving, pinches on a press — the wave starting under it (`rippleAgentPress`) —, breathes a halo while it waits — where it stopped, as long as the agent is at work, its next move leaving from there —, and fades once the agent is done and has left it idle a while. Every action run on a node (`act`) that changes the page moves it first, so a component has nothing to do for it; one that only reads or shows — `rows`, `point`, `focus` — moves none (`readOnlyHint`): the hand means the agent acts, a piece shown is framed alone; a script acting by hand calls `await agentTools.cursor.moveTo(el)`. Without movement, it fades from one target to the next. Beside it, what the agent says it is doing — « Archiving the report » —, a tag of the accent going with the dot (`agentTools.cursor.say(text)`, `moveTo(el, { label })`): the agent writes it in the optional `label` of `act`, in the page's language; a call without one takes the tag away.
- That an agent is at work is said over the whole window while it lasts (`AgentPresence`, in the loader; `shapes/agent-presence`): a line of the accent along the window's edges, breathing, and at its foot a pill — « Agent at work » — with the way to stop it, the only part the pointer reaches. Each tool call holds it while it runs (`AgentToolsService.run()`), a chat while it awaits the agent's answer (`abstract-agent-chat`); it goes a moment after the last hold, so a turn's calls do not blink it. **Stop** is the user's: `agentTools.stop()` lets the presence go, says `agent:stop` on the document, and refuses every call until the user writes again (`resume()`, sent by the chat with their next message). Anything else knowing an agent works holds it under a key of its own: `agentTools.presence.hold(key)` / `release(key)`.
- The controls demo (*Menus*) shows an entry pressed, one pointed at, and one pressed by an agent; *Agent* plays an agent's turn — pointing, pressing, typing — under its presence, and stops with it.

## The chat

Three layers, and two bricks under them. `abstract-entity-thread` is the thread alone: the messages of a conversation as a collection read backwards — the latest at the foot, older ones loaded above —, what arrives on the live topic, the wait for an answer, the layout, the lines folded together; it asks two things of a line, `isOwnMessage` and `isSpoken` — someone speaking, as against a line the thread carries for its machinery —, both true by default. It does not write. `composer` is the field alone: the words, the files going with them, the button sending them, what can be done under the field (`actions`), a `panel` slot under that; it knows nothing of where a message goes and is handed `submit(content, files)` by whoever does, the field emptied once it returns, kept if it throws. `abstract-entity-chat` is a thread one writes to: the two together, and over them what belongs to the conversation itself — the sound a message makes, which of the chats open on it listens —, sending what the composer hands it as the entity it is the collection of or as a slash command. A chat between people stands on it; a screen composed of zones puts a thread in one and a composer in another, each a component of its own on the brick it needs, the two speaking through whatever holds them.

`abstract-agent-chat`, over it, is a chat talked to by an agent acting in the page: it runs the tool calls of whatever engine writes in it, hides the lines nobody reads, holds the answers across a page a call left, and sends what the user did along with what they write. A chat says, in the words of its own entity, what a call is, what an answer is, and where to send one:

| Hook | symfony-ai-conversation `message-chat` | Syrtis `agent-chat` |
|---|---|---|
| `readToolCall(entity)` | `type === 'tool'`, json in `body` | `contentType === 'tool_call'`, json in `content` |
| `isToolResult(entity)` | `callId` in the body | `name === 'ASYNC_FRONTEND_ACTION_COMPLETE'` |
| `postToolResult(call, callId, results, userEvents)` | `POST message/create` with `toolResult` | the same |
| `postAgentMessage(content, userEvents)` | the entity written | `POST message/create` with `content` and `userEvents` |
| `getConversationKey()` | the session id | the session id |

The chat draws a call as *Tool · name*, hides the answers, keeps the wait for the engine's reply across the lines a tool adds, and joins to each answer what the page told the user in toasts meanwhile (`notifications`). What the other side writes is drawn from markdown, raw html shown as text (`MarkdownHelper`). A chat talked to by an agent says `hasSound()`: a sound then says a message arrived, the reader free to turn it off from the bar above the thread; a chat between people makes none. While it lasts, a bar at the foot of the thread says the other side is at work: a mark turning, « Thinking… », how long it has been (`13m5s`) — and goes once it answered. The wait for an answer lasts until the other side is done — by default a message of theirs, or whatever the chat reads as the end in `isReplyComplete(payload)`, an event closing the request, `getLiveThread()` naming every event it listens for. The lines are laid flat by default, every one at the start with its author first; `layout: 'aligned'` puts the lines sent at the end side and the ones received at the start, each on four fifths of the thread, as a messenger does — whatever component draws them, the layout placing the line (`thread--item`) and not what is in it; `--thread-aligned-width` widens the lines where there is room. Files go with a message where `acceptsAttachments()` says so (`getAttachmentAccept()`, `getAttachmentLimit()`): a paperclip by the send button and files dropped on the composer, listed above the field as `file-chip`s; a message may be files alone, and `postMessage(content, files)` receives the browser's `File`s — the default writes the text alone. Under the composer, what can be done to the conversation itself: `getComposerActions()` → `[{ key, icon, label, onClick | href, confirm: { title, message }, flat, iconOnly, end, pressed }]`, flat compact buttons, none by default; `onClick(chat)` is awaited, the others disabled meanwhile, `confirm` asked first. `reloadEntitiesCollection()` reads the thread again from nothing, dropping what it held — a conversation emptied on the server. A thread read and found empty shows `getEmptyText()` in its middle — what the conversation is about, how to start it —, none by default, gone with the first message. Consecutive lines of the same `getMessageGroupKey(entity)` — `null`, the default, leaves a line alone — are gathered under one folded line (`disclosure`), « N messages » (`getMessageGroupLabel(group)`), closed unless `isMessageGroupOpen(group)`; each still drawn as any line, the group keyed by its first so one growing live stays open. Each line is drawn by the component `getMessageComponent(entity)` names — `thread-message`, the line of conversation, by default — handed `getMessageProps(entity)`, `{ message }` by default (the projection `getMessage(entity)`): a chat whose lines are something else, an inspector's block, returns its own component and passes it what it reads, `{ entity }` typically.

## What the user did

The components say what the user does to them, for the agent to read at its next turn (symfony-loader, *What the user did meanwhile*): a switch flipped, a field edited — once, when the user is done with it —, a button pressed, a table searched, sorted or filtered, an item chosen in a menu, a page of a list reached, a form sent. The chat takes the log with whatever it sends next, through `postMessage(content, userEvents)` and `postToolResult(call, callId, results, userEvents)`, and hides the lines the server writes for it (`isUserEvent`); Syrtis's `agent-chat` sends them in the same request as the message, each stamped `user_event`.

## Over a modal

One conversation, shown twice. The dock's chat stands behind a modal's veil; the tools of the dock marked `overlay: true` — the chat — stand again over the veil, at the window's end (`overlay-tools`, drawn by the dashboard layout), the modal or the panel moving over to leave them room. One chat at a time listens to the conversation: the one in front, or the first opened; the one handed it back reads what it missed. The agent sees none of this: it reads the page, the overlay being a node like another.
